  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Vertically stacked low pressure is \ncentered over MN this afternoon with a warm front well to our north \nacross northern Ontario into Quebec. Trailing cold front sits to our \nwest, draped from the western U.P. to southern Lake Michigan into \nthe TN Valley. This boundary will make headway into northern MI this \nevening, clearing east of the area tonight. Secondary cold front \nexpected to arrive late in the day Thursday before this system \nshifts east entirely by Friday morning. All in all, occasionally \nunsettled weather expected through the remainder of the work week \nbefore high pressure settles in this weekend. A couple of northern \nstream waves possible early-mid next week, which may graze the area \nwith additional precip chances.\n\nForecast Details: Periods of showers are expected to continue this \nafternoon, with at least low chances for a few embedded pre-frontal \nthunderstorms given the expectation of a couple hundred J/kg MLCAPE \nin place by later this afternoon/early evening. While not expected \nto be severe, locally and brief gusty winds in/near thunderstorms \nmay result. Otherwise, showery conditions are expected to persist at \ntimes through the day Thursday as low pressure slowly drifts across \nthe northern Great Lakes.\n\nWhile Thursday will be a good 15-20 degrees cooler than today, even \ncolder temperatures are expected to filter in post secondary cold \nfrontal passage Thursday evening. Lingering synoptic support \ncombined with increasing over-lake instability will likely result in \nadditional lake-enhanced showers Thursday night. Latest trends \nsupport rain may mix with or change over to a bit of wet snow, \nespecially across portions of the eastern U.P. and interior higher \nterrain of northern lower. Suppose minor snow accumulation is \npossible in some areas (0.5\" or less), but ground temperatures \nshould still be pretty warm to limit this. By midday Friday, any \nremaining showers should be back to all rain with decreasing \nconfidence heading through Friday night as we should eventually be \nwarm advecting, likely bringing an end to any lingering lake effect \nshower activity. Just how quickly this shuts off remains the \nquestion, and if it extends far enough into the evening, suppose \nthat rain could mix back with a bit of wet snow -- again primarily \nlimited to inland areas.\n\nTrending precipication-free for at least a good chunk of the weekend \ninto early next week. High temps Saturday into the 40s rise back \ninto the 50s for Sunday and Monday. Potential exists for a couple of \nnorthern stream waves to scrape the area, perhaps as early as \nSunday/Monday and again towards the middle of next week. Precip \nchances with these waves look low, but non-zero.",
